-- title:
--   Finite surjective degeneracy map of two-chart models of X₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M \neq 0$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$, of the $q$-expansion field [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (Gamma1 (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101). Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with an algebra structure over which $L$ is its fraction field, with $p$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ in the image of $A \to L$, and let $K$ be an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with image in $L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion $q^{-1} \cdot \sum_n a_n q^n$ of the modular invariant. Let $KM$, $jM$ be the corresponding data at level $M$, i.e. $KM =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField M)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) with $jM$ nonzero having the same $q$-expansion, and assume $KM \le K$. Write, for a field $F$ as above with a distinguished $j$, $\mathrm{chartAlgFin} =$ the subalgebra of elements of $F$ integral over $A[j]$, $\mathrm{chartAlgInf} =$ that of elements integral over $A[j^{-1}]$, and [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) for the pushout (glueing) of the two affine schemes $\operatorname{Spec}$ of these algebras along the middle chart, with `modelTo` its structural morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and `ιFin`, `ιInf` the two chart immersions. The assertion is that there exist a morphism of schemes $f$ from the two-chart model attached to $(A, K, j)$ to the one attached to $(A, KM, jM)$, together with $A$-algebra maps $\iota_F$ on the finite charts and $\iota_I$ on the infinite charts, from level $M$ to level $Mp$, such that: $\iota_F$ and $\iota_I$ are the identity on $q$-expansions, i.e. each $b$ and its image have the same image in $L((q))$; $f$ followed by `modelTo` at level $M$ equals `modelTo` at level $Mp$, so $f$ is a morphism over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; `ιFin` at level $Mp$ followed by $f$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\iota_F)$ followed by `ιFin` at level $M$, and likewise for the infinite charts and $\iota_I$; the $f$-preimage of the open range of each chart immersion at level $M$ is the open range of the corresponding chart immersion at level $Mp$; and $f$ is a finite morphism with surjective underlying map of topological spaces.
--
--   This is the level-lowering (forgetful) degeneracy morphism $X_1(Mp) \to X_1(M)$ realised on the two-chart integral models over the valuation ring $A$ of $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$ above $p$, packaged together with the chart-level algebra maps, the compatibility of the chart opens and the finiteness and surjectivity of $f$. It is obtained by instantiating the general functoriality of the two-chart integral model along a module-finite inclusion of function fields, here $KM \subseteq K$, and is used in the subsequent analysis of reductions of points and Picard-group data on the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$.
